<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part VI &mdash; Seams, Horizons and Time &middot; Chapters 11 to 15</p>
  <h1>Seams, Horizons &amp; Time</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A seam is where one level ends and the next begins &mdash; and the framework's
    claim is that it is always the same seam, appearing at whatever scale you happen to be looking.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 11</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam">One Seam, Many Scales</h2>
    <p class="lede">Wherever the framework found a transition it found the same structure: a boundary
      that is not a wall but an identification, where what was outside becomes inside.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>At the quantum scale, <a href="/moebius/#t-collapse">collapse</a>: two faces of possibility
        becoming one face of fact.</li>
      <li>At the stellar scale, the event horizon &mdash; the subject of the next chapter.</li>
      <li>At the scale of a life, the moment of recognition at which the spiral is seen to have been a
        spiral.</li>
      <li>At the scale of the whole, the saturation ceiling: the point at which a density is full and can
        hold nothing more without inverting.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-horizon">Chapter 12 &mdash; The Leaking Horizon</h3>
      <p>Hawking radiation is real physics: a horizon is not perfectly black, and a hole slowly
        evaporates. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The framework reads the horizon as a seam, and the
        radiation as <strong>density leakage</strong> &mdash; the seam failing to hold perfectly, letting
        a trickle back across the boundary it defines.</p>
      <p class="muted">On the information question it is deliberately brief, and does not claim to settle
        the paradox. A seam that leaks is a picture; unitarity is a constraint. The chapter says which it
        is offering.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-octave">Chapter 13 &mdash; The Octave of the Cell</h3>
      <p>Interphase through mitosis is read against the seven stations, with division as the completed
        octave: one becoming two at the eighth step, the same note at double the frequency. The
        Fibonacci connection from <a href="/spiral/#t-splice">the splice</a> is the same law appearing in
        the lattice the cell is built from. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">Cell-cycle biology is well measured and does not need this reading; the chapter's
        claim is that the reading costs nothing and unifies something. That is a weaker claim than it
        first appears, and is filed as chosen.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 14</p>
    <h2 id="t-moment">The Passing Moment, Without Softening</h2>
    <p>If the self is the integral of its moments, then each moment, once integrated, is spent. The
      chapter refuses to soften the tension: the past is not stored somewhere intact, and nothing in the
      framework promises that it is.</p>
    <p>What it offers instead is a reframing &mdash; <strong>residue as relationship, observation as
      amplitude</strong>. A moment leaves a residue in the way a pole leaves a residue: not the thing
      itself, but a quantity the rest of the function must account for. What it costs is any comfortable
      doctrine of preservation. What it saves is consistency with
      <a href="/cosmic-ledger/">the Ledger</a>, where residues are exactly what remains of a singularity
      once you have integrated around it. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 15 &mdash; a revision the framework makes against itself</p>
    <h2 id="t-returners">The Returners Pass Below One</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The earlier chapters sent the returning being back to 1, leaving the ground
      untouched. That was tidy, and it wasted the foundation's best idea.</p>
  </div>

