<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the seam, at the scale of a charge</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam-small">The Smallest Seam Anyone Has Measured</h2>
    <p>This chapter has argued that one seam recurs at every scale: the horizon of a black hole, the
      threshold at one, the moment of passing. The claim is cheap unless the seam shows up somewhere small
      enough to be instrumented. It does, and it has a name that has nothing to do with this framework:
      confinement.</p>
    <p>Colour charge is never observed alone. What reaches a detector is always a colour bound to its own
      inversion &mdash; a quark with an antiquark, or three quarks taking one of each &mdash; and pulling
      the pair apart does not separate them; past about a femtometre the stored energy makes a fresh pair
      instead. The two faces cannot be prised off one another, which is exactly the property that
      distinguishes a one-sided surface from a two-sided one.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">A horizon is a seam you can fall through. Confinement is a seam you cannot get behind.
      The framework reads them as the same join at two scales, and the argument is laid out properly at
      <a href="/moebius/#t-colour">the seam and the colour charge</a>.</p>
    <p class="muted">The caution that belongs with it: QCD explains confinement with a running coupling and
      a flux tube, quantitatively, on a lattice, to several significant figures. The framework explains it
      with a topology and no number. Where a complete account already exists, a geometric restatement is a
      reading, not a rival, and it is logged as one.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:seams:end -->

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 31</p>
    <h2 id="t-arrow">Where the Arrow Comes From</h2>
    <p>The asymmetry noticed on the very first page &mdash; that the interval below one is denser than
      everything above it &mdash; is cashed in here. If there is more room in the direction of the small,
      then the natural drift of anything free to move is downhill toward the denser infinity. Time's
      direction is the slope of that tilt. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">The obvious objection is that thermodynamics already explains the arrow by counting
      states, and does so quantitatively. The framework's reply &mdash; that the counting itself inherits
      its asymmetry from the geometry &mdash; is taken up properly in
      <a href="/stress-tests/#t-entropy">the entropy objection</a>, where it is also conceded that this
      is the framework's weakest joint.</p>
    <p class="lede"><strong>And the joint has since been tested to destruction.</strong> The argument in
      the paragraph above &mdash; more room that way, therefore drift that way &mdash; was built as the
      smallest possible simulation and run. It fails. A walk on a tree with more room below does drift
      downward and does produce entropy, but only because the model samples uniformly over states;
      sample uniformly over directions instead and the drift goes to zero with every cardinality in the
      model untouched. The slope is not in the asymmetry. It is in the measure, and the framework has no
      argument for the measure. The run is at
      <a href="/stress-tests/#t-toy">Experiment five, the toy integrator</a>.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Put in the language of the thesis sentence: the pendulum is not swinging between two equal
      infinities. One side has more room in it than the other &mdash; strictly more, by Cantor's theorem
      &mdash; so the swing is biased, and a biased undamped swing has a direction without ever having a
      destination. That is the arrow as the framework first told it, and the
      <a href="/foundations/#t-ei-infinities">foundations</a> state both the theorem and the step taken
      beyond it. What the simulation adds is that the word <em>biased</em> is doing work no theorem
      supports: strictly more room on one side does not by itself make the swing prefer that side. Until
      something supplies the measure, this chapter states a hope with a proof attached to the wrong half
      of it. It is left standing, marked, because deleting a chapter is a poorer record than correcting
      it. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Next: <a href="/stress-tests/">Part VII &mdash; stress tests and open
      challenges</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
